   "failure_scenario": "A coordinator releases ownership but loses the response, and another coordinator acquires the next generation. The first coordinator still reports that its old write permission is valid, allowing a caller to act under obsolete ownership.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test \"$TMPDIR/lease-adjudication/regressions.test.ts\"",
    "output": "Using HEAD's fixture with the supplied release regressions:\nF1 unconfirmed {\"successorGeneration\":2,\"localConfirmed\":\"held\",\"windowOpen\":true}\nF1 unchanged {\"successorGeneration\":2,\"localConfirmed\":\"held\",\"windowOpen\":true}\nBoth assertions failed: Expected false, Received true."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD's open() checks only local confirmed ownership and the retained anchor: `if (!current(h) || a?.tip !== store.leaseRow(h.repo, h.ref)?.confirmed?.tip) return false; return windowOpen(a?.anchor ?? null, clock.sample());`. Release does not invalidate that anchor. `if (result.kind === \"unconfirmed\") return wait;` preserves it, and the unchanged path drops the intent without closing the window. These manager operations are entirely new versus base; the existing claim client already permits an ambiguous send to return unchanged after reading the previous tip. The later commit invalidates the anchor synchronously when release is invoked and uses a revocation token to prevent earlier writes from reopening it."
  },
  "B": {
