   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "After a daemon restart, a lease that SQLite records as confirmed and held (including one that recovery just confirmed) is never renewed by the renewal loop. The loop only knows about handles this process acquired, so the lease expires unless the caller acquires it again.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "same temp copy: bun test ./leases.test.ts -t \"review regression: restart renews\"",
    "output": "renew writes on tick: false 0\nExpected length: 2 Received length: 1\nrenew writes on tick: true 0\nExpected length: 1 Received length: 0\n(fail) x2"
   },
   "evidence": "`const handles = new Map<string, LeaseHandle>();` (49) is filled only by `handles.set(key(repo, ref), held)` after this instance's own applied write (192). The tick runs `for (const h of handles.values()) if (current(h) && next.active(h)) await manager.update(h, {});` (242), so rows from before the restart, and rows recovery confirmed, are skipped. Why minor: the request says resume and run-start paths call `acquire` (takeover false), which renews the lease and registers a handle, and a pending acquisition's caller got `wait` and would retry `acquire`. The gap only costs the lease if the wiring relies on the loop without acquiring again. The spec's wording ('update each confirmed held handle') is still not fully met."
  },
